positive_text:
Define a positive integer $ n $ as *super special* if there exist positive integers $ x, y, u, v $ such that $ n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 + 2v^3} $. Show that:
(a) An infinite number of super special integers exist;
(b) 2014 cannot be expressed in this form.

negatives.text:
A positive integer $n$ is called *super special* if it can be represented in the form $n = \frac{x^3 - 2y^3}{u^3 + 2v^3}$ for some positive integers $x, y, u, v$. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

A positive integer $n$ is called *super special* if it can be represented in the form $n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 - 2v^3}$ for some positive integers $x, y, u, v$. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

A positive integer $n$ is called *super special* if it can be represented in the form $n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 + 2v^3} + 1$ for some positive integers $x, y, u, v$. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

positive_text:
Let $n$ be an integer with $n \geqslant 3$. Suppose there are $n$ caves, each connected by a unique direct path to every other cave. Each campaign animal performs a round trip visiting every other cave exactly once, using only the direct paths and never traversing the same path twice. Moreover, no path is used by more than one campaign animal.

a) Show that if $n$ is a prime number, then the maximum number of campaign animals is $\frac{n-1}{2}$;

b) Determine the maximum number of campaign animals when $n = 9$.

negatives.text:
In a forest each of $n$ animals ($n \geqslant 3$) lives in its own cave, and there is exactly one separate path between any two of these caves. Before the election for King of the Forest some of the animals make an election campaign. Each campaign-making animal visits each of the other caves exactly once, uses only the paths for moving from cave to cave, never turns from one path to another between the caves and returns to its own cave in the end of its campaign. It is also known that no path between two caves is used by more than one campaign-making animal.

a) Prove that for any prime $n$, the maximum possible number of campaign-making animals is $\frac{n}{2}$;

b) Find the maximum number of campaign-making animals for $n=9$.

In a forest each of $n$ animals ($n \geqslant 3$) lives in its own cave, and there is exactly one separate path between any two of these caves. Before the election for King of the Forest some of the animals make an election campaign. Each campaign-making animal visits each of the other caves exactly once, uses only the paths for moving from cave to cave, never turns from one path to another between the caves and returns to its own cave in the end of its campaign. It is also known that no path between two caves is used by more than one campaign-making animal.

a) Prove that for any prime $n$, the maximum possible number of campaign-making animals is $\frac{n-1}{2} + 1$;

b) Find the maximum number of campaign-making animals for $n=9$.

In a forest each of $n$ animals ($n \geqslant 3$) lives in its own cave, and there is exactly one separate path between any two of these caves. Before the election for King of the Forest some of the animals make an election campaign. Each campaign-making animal visits each of the other caves exactly once, uses only the paths for moving from cave to cave, never turns from one path to another between the caves and returns to its own cave in the end of its campaign. It is also known that no path between two caves is used by more than one campaign-making animal.

a) Prove that for any prime $n$, the maximum possible number of campaign-making animals is $\frac{n-2}{2}$;

b) Find the maximum number of campaign-making animals for $n=9$.

positive_text:
A committee consists of 25 countries and follows these rules:

(1) Meetings occur every day;
(2) Each meeting must include at least one country;
(3) No two meetings have the exact same set of countries present; and
(4) For any meeting on day $n$, the set of countries attending must share at least one country with the set from each earlier meeting on day $k$, where $k < n$.

What is the maximum number of days the committee can meet?

negatives.text:
The 25 member states of the European Union set up a committee with the following rules:

(1) the committee should meet daily;

(2) at each meeting, at least one member state should be represented;

(3) at any two different meetings, a different set of member states should be represented; and

(4) at the $n$'th meeting, for every $k < n$, the set of states represented should include at least one state that was NOT represented at the $k$'th meeting.

For how many days can the committee have its meetings?

The 25 member states of the European Union set up a committee with the following rules:

(1) the committee should meet daily;

(2) at each meeting, at least one member state should be represented;

(3) at any two different meetings, a different set of member states should be represented; and

(4) at the $n$'th meeting, for every $k < n$, the set of states represented should be a subset of the set represented at the $k$'th meeting.

For how many days can the committee have its meetings?

The 25 member states of the European Union set up a committee with the following rules:

(1) the committee should meet daily;

(2) at each meeting, at least one member state should be represented;

(3) at any two different meetings, a different set of member states should be represented; and

(4) at the $n$'th meeting, for every $k < n$, the set of states represented should include at least one state that was represented at the $k$'th meeting or at the $k+1$'th meeting.

For how many days can the committee have its meetings?

positive_text:
Suppose $a$, $b$, and $c$ are non-zero real numbers with $b > 0$, and that the quadratic equation $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ shares two distinct roots with the cubic equation $x^3 + bx^2 + ax + c = 0$. Show that

a) $abc > 16$;

b) $abc \ge \frac{3125}{108}$.

negatives.text:
Given real non-zero numbers $a$, $b$, $c$ ($b > 0$) so that two distinct roots of the equation $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ are also the roots of the equation $x^3 - bx^2 + ax + c = 0$. Prove that

a) $abc > 16$;

b) $abc \ge \frac{3125}{108}$.

Given real non-zero numbers $a$, $b$, $c$ ($b > 0$) so that two distinct roots of the equation $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ are also the roots of the equation $x^3 + bx^2 + a^2x + c = 0$. Prove that

a) $abc > 16$;

b) $abc \ge \frac{3125}{108}$.

Given real non-zero numbers $a$, $b$, $c$ ($b > 0$) so that two distinct roots of the equation $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ are also the roots of the equation $x^3 + bx^2 + ax + c = 0$. Prove that

a) $abc < 16$;

b) $abc \le \frac{3125}{108}$.

positive_text:
Consider a $n \times n$ grid, where each cell initially contains a beetle. At noon, each beetle moves to an adjacent cell (sharing a side). Determine the maximum number of cells that can be left unoccupied after the movement for

a) $n = 8$;

b) $n = 9$.

negatives.text:
Find all real numbers $a$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions:
1) $f(f(x)) = x f(x) + a x$ for all real $x$;
2) $f$ is not constant;
3) $f$ takes the value $a$.

Find all real numbers $a$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions:
1) $f(f(x)) = x f(x) - a x^2$ for all real $x$;
2) $f$ is not constant;
3) $f$ takes the value $a$.

Find all real numbers $a$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions:
1) $f(f(x)) = x f(x) - a x$ for all real $x$;
2) $f$ is not constant;
3) $f$ takes the value $a^2$.

