positive_text:
\text{(\alpha) For any positive real number } a, \text{ show that } a^3 - 3a + 2 \ge 0.
\text{(\beta) For all positive real numbers } a, b, c, \text{ prove that: }\quad\frac{a^2 b}{c} + \frac{b^2 c}{a} + \frac{c^2 a}{b} + 2 \left( \frac{b}{ac} + \frac{c}{ab} + \frac{a}{bc} \right) \ge 9.\quad (1)
\text{Under what condition does equality occur?}

negatives.text:
\text{(\alpha) For every real number } x > 0 \text{ prove that: } x^3 - 3x \ge -2.
\text{(\beta) For all real numbers } x, y, z > 0, \text{ prove that: }\quad \frac{x^2 y}{z} + \frac{y^2 z}{x} + \frac{z^2 x}{y} + 2 \left( \frac{y}{x^2 z} + \frac{z}{x y^2} + \frac{x}{y z^2} \right) \ge 9. \quad (1)
\text{When equality holds?}

positive_text:
Let $n > 1$ be an integer. Determine all binary sequences $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n(n+1)}$ such that:
(i) $b_{j} \in \{0,1\}$ for all $1 \leq j \leq n(n+1)$;
(ii) for every integer $k$ with $0 \leq k \leq n(n-1)$, the sum $\sum_{j=1}^{n} b_{k+j}$ is strictly less than $\sum_{j=n+1}^{2n} b_{k+j}$.

negatives.text:
For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ let $d(n)$ denote the number of (positive) divisors of $n$. Find all functions $f: \mathbb{N} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ with the following properties:
(i) $d(f(x))=x$ for all $x \in \mathbb{N}$;
(ii) $f(x y)$ divides $(x+1) y^{x y-1} f(x)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{N}$.

For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ let $d(n)$ denote the number of (positive) divisors of $n$. Find all functions $f: \mathbb{N} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ with the following properties:
(i) $d(f(x))=x$ for all $x \in \mathbb{N}$;
(ii) $f(x y)$ divides $(x-1) y^{x y} f(x)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{N}$.

For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ let $d(n)$ denote the number of (positive) divisors of $n$. Find all functions $f: \mathbb{N} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ with the following properties:
(i) $d(f(x))=x$ for all $x \in \mathbb{N}$;
(ii) $f(x y)$ divides $(x-1) y^{x y-1} f(x)^2$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{N}$.

positive_text:
In triangle $ABC$, let $R$ be the circumradius, and let $a, b, c$ be the side lengths $BC, CA, AB$, respectively. Denote by $r_{a}, r_{b}, r_{c}$ the exradii opposite $A, B, C$. Given that $2R \leq r_{a}$, show that
(i) $a$ is the largest side;
(ii) $2R > r_{b}$ and $2R > r_{c}$.

Let $g$ be a function from the set $\{(a, b): a, b$ reals, $a b \neq 0\}$ to the set of positive real numbers satisfying
(i) $\quad g(a b, c) = g(a, c) g(b, c)$, for all $a, b \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad g(a, b c) = g(a, b) g(a, c)$, for all $a, b \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad g(a, 1 - a) = 1$, for all $a \neq 0, 1$.
Show that
(a) $\quad g(a, a) = g(a, -a) = 1$, for all $a \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad g(a, b) g(b, a) = 1$, for all $a, b \neq 0$.

negatives.text:
Let $f$ be a function defined from the set $\{(x, y): x, y$ reals, $x y \neq 0\}$ into the set of all positive real numbers such that
(i) $\quad f(x y, z)=f(x, z) + f(y, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad f(x, y z)=f(x, y) + f(x, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad f(x, 1-x)=1$, for all $x \neq 0,1$.
Prove that
(a) $\quad f(x, x)=f(x,-x)=1$, for all $x \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad f(x, y) f(y, x)=1$, for all $x, y \neq 0$.

Let $f$ be a function defined from the set $\{(x, y): x, y$ reals, $x y \neq 0\}$ into the set of all positive real numbers such that
(i) $\quad f(x y, z)=f(x, z) f(y, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad f(x, y z)=f(x, y) f(x, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad f(x, 1+x)=1$, for all $x \neq 0,1$.
Prove that
(a) $\quad f(x, x)=f(x,-x)=1$, for all $x \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad f(x, y) f(y, x)=1$, for all $x, y \neq 0$.

Let $f$ be a function defined from the set $\{(x, y): x, y$ reals, $x y \neq 0\}$ into the set of all positive real numbers such that
(i) $\quad f(x y, z)=f(x, z) f(y, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad f(x, y z)=f(x, y) f(x, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad f(x, 1-x)=2$, for all $x \neq 0,1$.
Prove that
(a) $\quad f(x, x)=f(x,-x)=1$, for all $x \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad f(x, y) f(y, x)=1$, for all $x, y \neq 0$.

positive_text:
\text{Let } P(x) = Q(y) \text{ be an equation where } P, Q \in \mathbb{Z}[x] \text{ are polynomials of degree at least one. Such an equation is called interesting if there exist infinitely many pairs } (x, y) \in \mathbb{N}^2 \text{ such that } P(x) = Q(y). \text{ We say } F(x) = G(y) \text{ is a transformation of } P(x) = Q(y) \text{ if there exists } R(x) \in \mathbb{Q}[x] \text{ such that } F(x) = R(P(x)) \text{ and } G(y) = R(Q(y)).\\
\text{a) Given an infinite subset } S \subset \mathbb{N}^2 \text{ such that every element of } S \text{ satisfies some interesting equation, prove there exists an interesting equation } P_o(x) = Q_o(y) \text{ such that every other interesting equation satisfied by } S \text{ is a transformation of } P_o(x) = Q_o(y).\\
\text{b) Define the degree of an interesting equation as the maximum degree of } P \text{ and } Q. \text{ An interesting equation is primitive if it cannot be obtained as a transformation of another interesting equation with a strictly smaller degree. Prove that if an interesting equation } P(x) = Q(y) \text{ is primitive and both } P \text{ and } Q \text{ are monic polynomials, then the degrees of } P \text{ and } Q \text{ are coprime.}

negatives.text:
ext{The equation } P(x) = Q(y) 	ext{ is called interesting if both } P 	ext{ and } Q 	ext{ are polynomials with integer coefficients and degree at least one and the equation has finitely many solutions in natural numbers. We say that equation } F(x) = G(y) 	ext{ results from equation } P(x) = Q(y), 	ext{ if polynomial } R 	ext{ with rational coefficients exists such that } F(x) = R(P(x)) 	ext{ and } G(y) = R(Q(y)).

ext{a) Suppose that } S 	ext{ is an infinite subset of the set } \mathbb{N} 	imes \mathbb{N}. 	ext{ We say that } S 	ext{ satisfies the interesting equation } P(x) = Q(y), 	ext{ if each element of it satisfies this equation. Prove that an interesting equation } P_o(x) = Q_o(y) 	ext{ exists such that every equation that is satisfied by } S 	ext{ (if any), has resulted from the equation } P_o(x) = Q_o(y).

ext{b) The degree of an interesting equation is defined as the largest degree between the degrees of both } P 	ext{ and } Q. 	ext{ We call an interesting equation primitive if it is not resulted from any other interesting equation with a lesser degree. Prove that if } P(x) = Q(y) 	ext{ is a primitive interesting equation and both } P 	ext{ and } Q 	ext{ are monadic polynomials, then the degrees of } P 	ext{ and } Q 	ext{ are coprime natural numbers.}

positive_text:
\text{Let } p(x) \in \mathbb{R}[x] \text{ be a polynomial of odd degree } m \text{ with } m > 1. \text{ Suppose } f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{Z} \text{ satisfies } p(f(x)) = f(p(x)) \text{ for every real number } x.

a) \text{ Show that the image of } f \text{ is finite.}

b) \text{ If } f \text{ is not constant, prove that the equation } p(x) = x \text{ has at least two distinct real roots.}

c) \text{ For any integer } n > 1, \text{ construct a function } f \text{ with a finite range of size } n \text{ and a polynomial } p(x) \text{ such that all the given conditions hold.}

