(i) \text{ Show that } |P| \le m(P), \text{ with equality if and only if } \min(P) < |P|;
(ii) \text{ Show that } m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} - 1).

\text{For a finite non-empty set of primes } P, \text{ let } |P| \text{ be the size of the set } P, \text{ and } m(P) \text{ be the largest possible number of consecutive positive integers, each of which is divisible by at least one member of } P.
(i) \text{ Show that } |P| \le m(P), \text{ with equality if and only if } \min(P) > |P|;
(ii) \text{ Show that } m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} + 1).

\text{For a finite non-empty set of primes } P, \text{ let } |P| \text{ be the size of the set } P, \text{ and } m(P) \text{ be the largest possible number of consecutive positive integers, each of which is divisible by at least one member of } P.
(i) \text{ Show that } |P| \le m(P), \text{ with equality if and only if } \min(P) > |P|;
(ii) \text{ Show that } m(P) < (|P| - 1)(2^{|P|} - 1).

positive_text:
Determine whether there exists a polynomial $P(u, v)$ with integer coefficients and two distinct points $C = (c_1, c_2)$ and $D = (d_1, d_2)$ in the plane such that:
(i) $C$ has integer coordinates;
(ii) the Manhattan distance between $C$ and $D$ is 2010;
(iii) $P(m, n) > P(c_1, c_2)$ for all integer pairs $(m, n)$ different from $C$;
(iv) $P(u, v) > P(d_1, d_2)$ for all points $(u, v)$ in the plane different from $D$.

negatives.text:
Determine whether there exist a polynomial $f(x_1, x_2)$ in two variables, with integer coefficients, and two points $A = (a_1, a_2)$ and $B = (b_1, b_2)$ in the plane, satisfying all the following conditions
(i) $A$ is an integer point (i.e., $a_1$ and $a_2$ are integers);
(ii) $|a_1 - b_1| + |a_2 - b_2| = 2010$;
(iii) $f(n_1, n_2) < f(a_1, a_2)$, for all integer points $(n_1, n_2)$ in the plane other than $A$;
(iv) $f(x_1, x_2) > f(b_1, b_2)$, for all points $(x_1, x_2)$ in the plane other than $B$.

Determine whether there exist a polynomial $f(x_1, x_2)$ in two variables, with integer coefficients, and two points $A = (a_1, a_2)$ and $B = (b_1, b_2)$ in the plane, satisfying all the following conditions
(i) $A$ is an integer point (i.e., $a_1$ and $a_2$ are integers);
(ii) $|a_1 - b_1| + |a_2 - b_2| = 2010$;
(iii) $f(n_1, n_2) > f(a_1, a_2)$, for all integer points $(n_1, n_2)$ in the plane other than $A$;
(iv) $f(x_1, x_2) > f(b_1, b_2)$, for all integer points $(x_1, x_2)$ in the plane other than $B$.

Determine whether there exist a polynomial $f(x_1, x_2)$ in two variables, with integer coefficients, and two points $A = (a_1, a_2)$ and $B = (b_1, b_2)$ in the plane, satisfying all the following conditions
(i) $A$ is an integer point (i.e., $a_1$ and $a_2$ are integers);
(ii) $|a_1 - b_1| + |a_2 - b_2| = 2010$;
(iii) $f(n_1, n_2) > f(a_1, a_2)$, for all integer points $(n_1, n_2)$ in the plane other than $A$;
(iv) $f(x_1, x_2) < f(b_1, b_2)$, for all points $(x_1, x_2)$ in the plane other than $B$.

positive_text:
Let $ABCD$ be an isosceles trapezoid with $AC \perp BD$. A line is drawn through the intersection point $O$ of the diagonals, parallel to the bases, intersecting side $[BC]$ at $P$ and side $[AD]$ at $R$. Let $Q$ be the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $[BC]$. Prove that

a) the segment $QR$ is equal in length to $AD$;

b) the segment $QR$ is perpendicular to $AD$.

negatives.text:
Consider an isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ with perpendicular diagonals. The parallel from the intersection point of the diagonals meets the non-parallel sides $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at points $P$ and $R$ respectively. Point $Q$ is the mirror image of $P$ across the midpoint of $[AD]$. Show that

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

Consider an isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ with perpendicular diagonals. The parallel from the intersection point of the diagonals meets the non-parallel sides $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at points $P$ and $R$ respectively. Point $Q$ is the mirror image of $R$ across the midpoint of $[BC]$. Show that

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

Consider an isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ with perpendicular diagonals. The parallel from the intersection point of the diagonals meets the non-parallel sides $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at points $P$ and $R$ respectively. Point $Q$ is the mirror image of $P$ across the midpoint of $[BC]$. Show that

a) $QR = AB$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

positive_text:
Determine all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
1) $f(m+n) - f(m) - f(n) = 2mn$ for all integers $m, n$;
2) $f(f(x)) - f(1)$ is a perfect square for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$.

negatives.text:
Find all the functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ fulfilling the conditions:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)-2mn$, for every integers $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x))-f(1)$ is a perfect square for each integer $x$.

Find all the functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ fulfilling the conditions:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn$, for every integers $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x))+f(1)$ is a perfect square for each integer $x$.

Find all the functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ fulfilling the conditions:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn$, for every integers $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x))-f(1)$ is a perfect cube for each integer $x$.

positive_text:
Let $J \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $g : J \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

a) $g(a) - g(b) < |a - b|$, for all $a, b \in J$;

b) $\int_a^b g(s)ds - g(a)(b - a) \le \frac{1}{2}(a - b)^2$, for all $a, b \in J$.

negatives.text:
Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y-x) < \frac{1}{2}(x-y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y-x) \le \frac{1}{2}(x-y)^3$, for every $x, y \in I$.

Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a decreasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $0$, then there exists a decreasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $x_n^2 \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a decreasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $0$, then there exists a decreasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$, and $y_n$ is also a strictly decreasing sequence.

positive_text:
Determine all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ that are differentiable and satisfy the following conditions:
1) $f'(n) = 0$ for every integer $n$;
2) for any real number $x$, if $f'(x) = 0$, then $f(x) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f'(x) = 0$, for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f(x) = 1$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f'(x) = 0$, for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f'(x) = 0$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f(x) = 0$, for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f(x) = 0$.

positive_text:
In a right triangle $ABC$ with $\angle B = 90^\circ$, let $I$ be the incenter. Let the incircle touch $AB$, $BC$, and $AC$ at points $F$, $D$, and $E$, respectively. If $CI$ intersects $EF$ at $M$, and $DM$ intersects $AB$ at $N$, prove that:

a) $ND = AI$;

b) $\frac{EI \cdot EM}{EC} = FM$.

negatives.text:
Consider a triangle $ABC$, such that $\angle B = 90^\circ$. Denote by $I$ the in-center and let $F$, $D$ and $E$ be the points where the incircle touches sides $[AB]$, $[BC]$, and $[AC]$ respectively. If $CI \cap EF = \{M\}$ and $DM \cap AB = \{N\}$, show that:
a) $AI = ND$;
b) $FM = \frac{EI \cdot EM}{EC^2}$.

Consider a triangle $ABC$, such that $\angle B = 90^\circ$. Denote by $I$ the in-center and let $F$, $D$ and $E$ be the points where the incircle touches sides $[AB]$, $[BC]$, and $[AC]$ respectively. If $CI \cap EF = \{M\}$ and $DM \cap AB = \{N\}$, show that:
a) $AI = ND$;
b) $FM = \frac{EI \cdot EM}{AE}$.

positive_text:
Let $ABC$ be a right isosceles triangle with $\angle A = 90^\circ$. Choose a point $M$ on side $BC$ such that $\angle AMB = 75^\circ$. Let $F$ lie on the internal bisector of $\angle MAC$ such that the segment $BF$ is equal in length to $AB$. Prove that:

a) the lines $AM$ and $BF$ are perpendicular;

b) triangle $CFM$ is isosceles.

negatives.text:
The right isosceles triangle $ABC$ has $m(\angle A) = 90^\circ$. Take $M \in (BC)$ so that $m(\angle AMB) = 105^\circ$ and take $F$ on the internal bisector of the angle $MAC$ so that $BF = AB$. Prove that:
a) the lines $AM$ and $BF$ are perpendicular;
b) the triangle $CFM$ is isosceles.

The right isosceles triangle $ABC$ has $m(\angle A) = 90^\circ$. Take $M \in (BC)$ so that $m(\angle AMB) = 75^\circ$ and take $F$ on the external bisector of the angle $MAC$ so that $BF = AB$. Prove that:
a) the lines $AM$ and $BF$ are perpendicular;
b) the triangle $CFM$ is isosceles.

The right isosceles triangle $ABC$ has $m(\angle A) = 90^\circ$. Take $M \in (BC)$ so that $m(\angle AMB) = 75^\circ$ and take $F$ on the internal bisector of the angle $MAC$ so that $CF = AB$. Prove that:
a) the lines $AM$ and $BF$ are perpendicular;
b) the triangle $CFM$ is isosceles.

If $A$ and $B$ are positive integers, then $\overline{AB}$ will denote the number obtained by writing, in order, the digits of $B$ after the digits of $A$. For instance, if $A = 193$ and $B = 2016$, then $\overline{AB} = 1932016$. Prove that there are infinitely many perfect cubes of the form $\overline{AB}$ in each of the following situations:
a) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;
b) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;
c) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;
d) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

If $A$ and $B$ are positive integers, then $\overline{AB}$ will denote the number obtained by writing, in order, the digits of $B$ after the digits of $A$. For instance, if $A = 193$ and $B = 2016$, then $\overline{AB} = 1932016$. Prove that there are infinitely many perfect squares of the form $\overline{AB}$ in each of the following situations:
a) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;
b) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;
c) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect cube;
d) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect square.

positive_text:
\text{a) S\u0103 se demonstreze c\u0103 } \frac{(a+b)^2}{2} + \frac{a + b}{4} \geq a \sqrt{b} + b \sqrt{a}, \text{ } a, b \geq 0

\text{b) S\u0103 se arate c\u0103 pentru } \forall x, y, z \in\left[0, \frac{\pi}{2}\right), \text{ are loc inegalitatea:}
\begin{aligned}
& (\tan x + \tan y)^2 + (\tan y + \tan z)^2 + (\tan z + \tan x)^2 + \tan x + \tan y + \tan z \\
& \geq 2\left(\tan x \sqrt{\tan y} + \tan y \sqrt{\tan x} + \tan y \sqrt{\tan z} + \tan z \sqrt{\tan y} + \tan z \sqrt{\tan x} + \tan x \sqrt{\tan z}\right)
\end{aligned}

Let $f, g: [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty)$ be continuous functions such that:
(i) $f(0) = g(0) = 0$;
(ii) $g(x) > 0$ for all $x > 0$;
(iii) $f(x) = f(x + g(f(x)))$ for all $x \ge 0$.
Prove that $f(x) = 0$ for all $x \ge 0$.

negatives.text:
The continuous functions $f, g: [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty)$ have the properties:
(i) $f(0) = g(0) = 0$;
(ii) $g(x) \neq 0$, for every $x > 0$;
(iii) $f(x + g(f(x))) = f(x) + 1$, for every $x \ge 0$.
Show that $f(x) = 0$, for every $x \ge 0$.

The continuous functions $f, g: [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty)$ have the properties:
(i) $f(0) = g(0) = 0$;
(ii) $g(x) \neq 0$, for every $x > 0$;
(iii) $f(x + g(f(x))) = f(x)^2$, for every $x \ge 0$.
Show that $f(x) = 0$, for every $x \ge 0$.

The continuous functions $f, g: [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty)$ have the properties:
(i) $f(0) = g(0) = 0$;
(ii) $g(x) \neq 0$, for every $x > 0$;
(iii) $f(x - g(f(x))) = f(x)$, for every $x \ge 0$.
Show that $f(x) = 0$, for every $x \ge 0$.

positive_text:
Let $g: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) for all sufficiently large $n$, i.e., $n > n_0$, there exists a unique $x_n > 0$ such that $\int_0^{x_n} g(t) \, dt = \frac{1}{n}$;
b) the sequence $(n x_n)_{n > n_0}$ converges and determine its limit.

negatives.text:
Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n > n_0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = 1$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
b) the sequence $(x_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.

Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n > n_0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = 1$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
b) the sequence $(n^2x_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.

Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n > n_0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = 1$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
b) the sequence $(\log(n)x_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.

positive_text:
Let $(x_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be a sequence of positive integers with common difference $d > 0$ and initial term $a$. Let $T_n = x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \dots + x_n^2$. Prove that:

a) if $p$ is a prime with $p \ge 5$, then $p \mid T_p$;

b) $T_5$ is not a perfect square.

negatives.text:
Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and let $S_n = a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_n$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Prove that:

a) if $p \ge 5$ is a prime number, then $p$ divides $S_p$;

b) $S_5$ is not a square.

Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and let $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Prove that:

a) if $p$ is an odd prime number, then $p$ divides $S_{p-1}$;

b) $S_5$ is not a square.

Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and let $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Prove that:

a) if $p \ge 5$ is a prime number, then $p$ divides $S_p$;

b) $S_5$ is a square.

positive_text:
Fie $n \in \mathbb{N}^{*}, n \geq 2$. Demonstrați că, pentru orice numere complexe $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ și $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$, următoarele afirmații sunt echivalente:
a) Inegalitatea $\sum_{k=1}^{n} \left| z - a_{k} \right|^2 \leq \sum_{k=1}^{n} \left| z - b_{k} \right|^2$ este valabilă pentru orice $z \in \mathbb{C}$;
b) $\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_{k} = \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_{k}$ și $\sum_{k=1}^{n} \left| a_{k} \right|^2 \leq \sum_{k=1}^{n} \left| b_{k} \right|^2$.

negatives.text:
Fie $n \in \mathbb{N}^{*}, n \geq 2$. Demonstrați că, pentru orice numere complexe $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ și $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$, următoarele afirmații sunt echivalente:

a) $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-a_{k}\right|^{2} < \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-b_{k}\right|^{2}$, pentru orice $z \in \mathbb{C}$;

b) $\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_{k}=\sum_{k=1}^{n} b_{k}$ și $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|a_{k}\right|^{2} < \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|b_{k}\right|^{2}$.

Fie $n \in \mathbb{N}^{*}, n \geq 2$. Demonstrați că, pentru orice numere complexe $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ și $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$, următoarele afirmații sunt echivalente:

a) $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-a_{k}\right|^{2} \leq \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-b_{k}\right|^{2}$, pentru orice $z \in \mathbb{C}$;

b) $\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_{k}=\sum_{k=1}^{n} b_{k}$ și $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|a_{k}\right|^{2} \geq \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|b_{k}\right|^{2}$.

Fie $n \in \mathbb{N}^{*}, n \geq 2$. Demonstrați că, pentru orice numere complexe $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ și $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$, următoarele afirmații sunt echivalente:

a) $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-a_{k}\right|^{2} \leq \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-b_{k}\right|^{2}$, pentru orice $z \in \mathbb{C}$;

b) $\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_{k}^{2}=\sum_{k=1}^{n} b_{k}^{2}$ și $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|a_{k}\right|^{2} \leq \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|b_{k}\right|^{2}$.

Let $ABCD$ be a square and $E$ an arbitrary point on the side $CD$. On the outside of the triangle $ABE$ one considers the squares $ENMA$ and $EBQP$. Prove that:

a) $ND = PB$;

b) $ND \perp PC$.

Let $ABCD$ be a square and $E$ an arbitrary point on the side $CD$. On the outside of the triangle $ABE$ one considers the squares $ENMA$ and $EBQP$. Prove that:

a) $ND = PC$;

b) $ND \parallel PC$.

positive_text:
Determine all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that:
(i) $f(f(a^2) + b + f(b)) = a^2 + 2f(b)$;
(ii) $a \le b$ implies $f(a) \le f(b)$;
for all real numbers $a$ and $b$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = -x^2 + 2f(y)$;
(ii) $x \le y$ implies $f(x) \le f(y)$;
for all real numbers $x$ and $y$.

Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = x^2 + 2f(y^2)$;
(ii) $x \le y$ implies $f(x) \le f(y)$;
for all real numbers $x$ and $y$.

Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = x^2 + 2f(y)$;
(ii) $x \le y$ implies $f(x) \ge f(y)$;
for all real numbers $x$ and $y$.

Arătaţi că, pentru orice număr natural $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu $m$ al său, nenul, cu următoarele proprietăţi:
a) $m < n^{4}$;
b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc cel mult trei cifre distincte.

Arătaţi că, pentru orice număr natural $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu $m$ al său, nenul, cu următoarele proprietăţi:
a) $m < 2n^{4}$;
b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc cel mult patru cifre distincte.

Arătaţi că, pentru orice număr natural $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu $m$ al său, nenul, cu următoarele proprietăţi:
a) $m < n^{4}$;
b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc exact patru cifre distincte.

positive_text:
Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function such that $f(f(x)) = x^{2023}$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$. Show that:
a) $\frac{f(x)}{x^{1/2023}} = \left(\frac{f(x^{1/2023})}{x^{1/2023}}\right)^{2023}$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
b) for any $n \ge 3$, there exists an arithmetic sequence $a, a + d, a + 2d, \dots, a + (n - 1)d$ such that $\sum_{k=0}^{n - 1} f(a + kd) = 0$.

negatives.text:
The continuous function $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ has the property $(f \circ f)(x) = -x^{2023}$, for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$. Prove that:
a) $2023\sqrt[2023]{f(x)} = f(2023\sqrt[2023]{x})$, for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
b) for every $n \ge 3$ there exists an arithmetic progression $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n$ so that $\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(x_k) = 0$.

The continuous function $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ has the property $(f \circ f)(x) = x^{2024}$, for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$. Prove that:
a) $2023\sqrt[2023]{f(x)} = f(2023\sqrt[2023]{x})$, for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
b) for every $n \ge 3$ there exists an arithmetic progression $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n$ so that $\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(x_k) = 0$.

The continuous function $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ has the property $(f \circ f)(x) = x^{2023}$, for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$. Prove that:
a) $2023\sqrt[2023]{f(x)} = f(2023\sqrt[2023]{x})$, for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
b) for every $n \ge 3$ there exists an arithmetic progression $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n$ so that $\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(x_k) = 1$.

positive_text:
Let $A, B \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$, and define a function $f: M_n(\mathbb{C}) \to M_n(\mathbb{C})$ by $f(Z) = AZ + B\bar{Z}$, for all $Z \in M_n(\mathbb{C})$, where $\bar{Z}$ denotes the matrix with complex conjugate entries of $Z$. Show that the following are equivalent:
(i) $f$ has a trivial kernel;
(ii) $f$ maps onto $M_n(\mathbb{C})$;
(iii) the matrices $A + B$ and $A - B$ are invertible.

negatives.text:
Consider $A, B \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$, and the function $f: M_n(\mathbb{C}) \to M_n(\mathbb{C})$, defined by $f(Z) = AZ + BZ$, $Z \in M_n(\mathbb{C})$. Prove that the following are equivalent:
(1) $f$ is injective;
(2) $f$ is surjective;
(3) matrices $A+B$ and $A-B$ are non-singular.

Consider $A, B \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$, and the function $f: M_n(\mathbb{C}) \to M_n(\mathbb{C})$, defined by $f(Z) = AZ - B\bar{Z}$, $Z \in M_n(\mathbb{C})$. Prove that the following are equivalent:
(1) $f$ is injective;
(2) $f$ is surjective;
(3) matrices $A+B$ and $A-B$ are non-singular.

Find all the real numbers $x$ and $y$ so that:
(i) $x \le 2y^2$;
(ii) $y \le 2x^2$;
(iii) the number $8(x - y)$ is an integer.

Find all the real numbers $x$ and $y$ so that:
(i) $x \ge 2y^2$;
(ii) $y \ge 2x^2$;
(iii) the number $8(x + y)$ is an integer.

Find all the real numbers $x$ and $y$ so that:
(i) $x \ge 2y^2$;
(ii) $y \ge 2x^2$;
(iii) the number $8(x - y)$ is a perfect square.

positive_text:
A positive integer $n$ is defined to be cool if $n = k^2$ for some integer $k$ and there exist distinct positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ with $a > b$ and $c > d$ such that $n = a^3 - b^3 + c^3 - d^3$. For example, $225 = 15^2 = 7^3 - 5^3 + 2^3 - 1^3$ is cool. Prove that:
a) $2025$ is cool;
b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

negatives.text:
We will call a positive integer $n$ cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, different from each other, with $a > b$ and $c > d$, such that $n = a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3$. For instance, $225$ is cool because $225 = 15^2$ and $225 = 7^3 + 5^3 + 2^3 + 1^3$. Show that:
a) $2025$ is cool;
b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

We will call a positive integer $n$ cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, different from each other, with $a > b$ and $c > d$, such that $n = a^2 - b^2 + c^2 - d^2$. For instance, $225$ is cool because $225 = 15^2$ and $225 = 7^2 - 5^2 + 2^2 - 1^2$. Show that:
a) $2025$ is cool;
b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

positive_text:
Suppose $(G, \cdot)$ is a group and $H < G$ is a proper subgroup. Assume there exist endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ such that for all $x, y \in G$ with $x, y \notin H$, it holds that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$. Prove that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

negatives.text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $H < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. If there are endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ of the group $G$, such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ holds for any $x, y \in H$, show that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $H < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. If there are endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ of the group $G$, such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ holds for any $x, y \in G$, show that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $H < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. If there are endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ of the group $G$, such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ holds for any $x, y \in G \setminus H$, show that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is abelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

positive_text:
Find prime numbers $p, q, r, s$, with $p \le q$ and $r \le s$, such that
(1) $p + q - r - s = 1$;
(2) $p^2 + q^2 + r^2 + s^2 = 3543$.

negatives.text:
Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d - 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543$.

Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543^2$.

Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3 = 3543$.

positive_text:
Let $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $AB + BA = A + B$. Show that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ are such that $A - B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ are such that $A + B = AB - BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ are such that $A + B = AB + B^2A$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

positive_text:
Suppose there are $n$ distinct languages used in a university. For each positive integer $k \leq n$, define $A_{k}$ as the set of individuals who can speak at least $k$ of these languages, and define $B_{k}$ as the set of individuals who, for any selection of $k$ distinct languages, can speak at least one of them. Prove that

a. If $2k \geq n+1$, then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$, then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

negatives.text:
The total number of languages used in KAUST is $n$. For each positive integer $k \leq n$, let $A_{k}$ be the set of all those people in KAUST who can speak at least $k$ languages; and let $B_{k}$ be the set of all people $P$ in KAUST with the property that, for any $k+1$ pairwise different languages (used in KAUST), $P$ can speak at least one of these $k+1$ languages. Prove that

a. If $2k \geq n+1$ then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$ then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

The total number of languages used in KAUST is $n$. For each positive integer $k \leq n$, let $A_{k}$ be the set of all those people in KAUST who can speak at most $k$ languages; and let $B_{k}$ be the set of all people $P$ in KAUST with the property that, for any $k$ pairwise different languages (used in KAUST), $P$ can speak at least one of these $k$ languages. Prove that

a. If $2k \geq n+1$ then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$ then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

positive_text:
Determine all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ such that:
1. For every real number $x$, $f(x+1) - f(x) \geq 1$;
2. For all real numbers $x$ and $y$, $f(x y) - f(x) f(y) \geq 0$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \leq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(xy) \geq f(x)f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \geq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(xy) \leq f(x)f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \geq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x^2) \geq f(x)^2$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

positive_text:
Нека је дата таблица $n \times n$ у којој су поља попуњена бројевима $1$ до $n^2$, тако да сваки број појављује тачно једном. Ако је за неко $n$ тачно да производ бројева на неком сету $n$ „непреклопљених" поља (тј. по једном из сваке врсти и колоне) увек дава остатак $r$ при дељењу са $n^2 + 1$, за неко фиксно $r$, кажемо да је таблица нишка. Постоје ли нишка таблице за:

а. $n=8$;

б. $n=10$ ?

Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that:
(i) each pair of distinct points is joined by a uniquely colored edge, with the color chosen from the set $\{R, G, B, Y\}$;
(ii) for any three distinct points, if the edges among them are not monochromatic, then they must all have distinct colors;
(iii) the set of edges is not monochromatic.
Determine the largest possible value of $n$.

negatives.text:
Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that:
(i) every two distinct points are connected by a string which is either red, green, blue, or yellow;
(ii) for every three distinct points, if the three strings between them are not all of the same colour, then they are of at least two different colours;
(iii) not all the strings have the same colour.
Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that:
(i) every two distinct points are connected by a string which is either red, green, blue, or yellow;
(ii) for every three distinct points, if the three strings between them are not all of the same colour, then they are of at most three different colours;
(iii) not all the strings have the same colour.
Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that:
(i) every two distinct points are connected by a string which is either red, green, blue, or yellow;
(ii) for every three distinct points, if the three strings between them are not all of the same colour, then they are of exactly two different colours;
(iii) not all the strings have the same colour.
Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

positive_text:
\text{Sea la desigualdad} \quad \left|x-1\right| < a x, \quad \text{con } a \in \mathbb{R}.
a) \text{Analizar la desigualdad para distintos valores de } a.
b) \text{Determinar los valores de } a \text{ tales que la desigualdad posee exactamente DOS soluciones enteras.}

