positive_text:
Es seien $x$, $y$, $z$, $w$ reelle Zahlen mit
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
x=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21-x}}, & y=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21-y}} \\
z=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21+z}}, & w=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21+w}}
\end{array}
$$
Beweise, dass $x y z w=2004$.

設 $x, y, z, w$ 為非負實數, 求解下列方程組:
$$
x^2(y+z)(y+z+w) = \sqrt{y+z}\sqrt[3]{y+z+w}
$$
$$
y^2(z+w)(z+w+x) = \sqrt{z+w}\sqrt[3]{z+w+x}
$$
$$
z^2(w+x)(w+x+y) = \sqrt{w+x}\sqrt[3]{w+x+y}
$$
$$
w^2(x+y)(x+y+z) = \sqrt{x+y}\sqrt[3]{x+y+z}
$$

negatives.text:
已知 $a, b, c, d$ 為非負實數, 試求滿足下列方程組的解 $(a, b, c, d)$:
$$
a^2(b+c)(b+c+d) = \sqrt{b+c}\sqrt[3]{(b+c+d)^2}
$$
$$
b^2(c+d)(c+d+a) = \sqrt{c+d}\sqrt[3]{(c+d+a)^2}
$$
$$
c^2(d+a)(d+a+b) = \sqrt{d+a}\sqrt[3]{(d+a+b)^2}
$$
$$
d^2(a+b)(a+b+c) = \sqrt{a+b}\sqrt[3]{(a+b+c)^2}
$$

已知 $a, b, c, d$ 為非負實數, 試求滿足下列方程組的解 $(a, b, c, d)$:
$$
a^2(b+c)(b-c+d) = \sqrt{b+c}\sqrt[3]{b-c+d}
$$
$$
b^2(c+d)(c-d+a) = \sqrt{c+d}\sqrt[3]{c-d+a}
$$
$$
c^2(d+a)(d-a+b) = \sqrt{d+a}\sqrt[3]{d-a+b}
$$
$$
d^2(a+b)(a-b+c) = \sqrt{a+b}\sqrt[3]{a-b+c}
$$

已知 $a, b, c, d$ 為非負實數, 試求滿足下列方程組的解 $(a, b, c, d)$:
$$
\frac{a^2(b+c)(b+c+d)}{b+c+d} = \sqrt{b+c}\sqrt[3]{b+c+d}
$$
$$
\frac{b^2(c+d)(c+d+a)}{c+d+a} = \sqrt{c+d}\sqrt[3]{c+d+a}
$$
$$
\frac{c^2(d+a)(d+a+b)}{d+a+b} = \sqrt{d+a}\sqrt[3]{d+a+b}
$$
$$
\frac{d^2(a+b)(a+b+c)}{a+b+c} = \sqrt{a+b}\sqrt[3]{a+b+c}
$$

positive_text:
\text{Let chords } AD \text{ and } BC \text{ of circle } O \text{ intersect at point } P. \text{ A circle } K \text{ is inscribed in the region bounded by } AP, PC \text{ and the arc of circle } O, \text{ and is tangent to } AD \text{ and } BC \text{ at } E \text{ and } F, \text{ respectively, and tangent to circle } O \text{ at } T. \text{ Line } TF \text{ meets circle } O \text{ again at } G, \text{ and line } AG \text{ meets } EF \text{ at } I. 
\text{Prove:}
(1) A, E, T, I \text{ lie on a circle.}
(2) GB = GI.

Prove that there exists a constant $ c > 0 $ such that infinitely many pairs $ (n, N) $ of positive integers satisfy:
(a) $ N > c \cdot 2^{\omega(N)} n \log n $, where $ \omega(N) $ denotes the number of distinct prime factors of $ N $;
(b) for every prime factor $ p $ of $ N $ with exponent $ e $ in the prime factorization of $ N $, the number $ n $ is a multiple of $ \varphi(p^e) $, where $ \varphi $ is Euler's totient function.

negatives.text:
Prove that there is a positive real number $c > 0$ such that there are infinitely many pairs $(n, N)$ of positive integers satisfying the following conditions:
(a) $N < c \cdot 2^k n \log(n)$, where $k$ is the number of distinct prime factors of $N$;
(b) $n$ is divisible by $\varphi(p^e)$ for every prime power $p^e$ exactly dividing $N$.

Prove that there is a positive real number $c > 0$ such that there are infinitely many pairs $(n, N)$ of positive integers satisfying the following conditions:
(a) $N > c \cdot 2^{k^2} n \log(n)$, where $k$ is the number of distinct prime factors of $N$;
(b) $n$ is divisible by $\varphi(p^e)$ for every prime power $p^e$ exactly dividing $N$.

Prove that there is a positive real number $c > 0$ such that there are infinitely many pairs $(n, N)$ of positive integers satisfying the following conditions:
(a) $N > c \cdot 2^k n \log(n)$, where $k$ is the number of distinct prime factors of $N$;
(b) $n$ is divisible by $p^e$ for every prime power $p^e$ exactly dividing $N$.

Determine all functions $ f $ defined on the set of all positive integers and taking non-negative integer values, satisfying the three conditions:
(1) $ f(n) \neq 0 $ for at least one $ n $;
(2) $ f(xy) = f(x) - f(y) $ for every positive integers $ x $ and $ y $;
(3) there are infinitely many positive integers $ n $ such that $ f(k) = f(n-k) $ for all $ k < n $.

Determine all functions $ f $ defined on the set of all positive integers and taking non-negative integer values, satisfying the three conditions:
(1) $ f(n) \neq 0 $ for at least one $ n $;
(2) $ f(xy) = f(x) + f(y) $ for every positive integers $ x $ and $ y $;
(3) there are infinitely many positive integers $ n $ such that $ f(k) = f(n+k) $ for all $ k < n $.

Determine all functions $ f $ defined on the set of all positive integers and taking non-negative integer values, satisfying the three conditions:
(1) $ f(n) \neq 0 $ for at least one $ n $;
(2) $ f(xy) = f(x) + f(y) $ for every positive integers $ x $ and $ y $;
(3) there are infinitely many positive integers $ n $ such that $ f(k) = f(n-k) $ for all $ k < n $ and $ f(n) = 0 $.

positive_text:
Consider all non-decreasing sequences $b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_{2008}$ with $b_k \in \{0, 1, \ldots, k-1\}$ for each $k$. Show that the number of such sequences is greater than

a) $2^{2007}$;

b) $2^{2008}$.

A country has $n$ cities, and a company operates bidirectional flights between some pairs of them. The flights form a connected graph where each city has the same degree. The company wants to remove as few edges as possible to make the graph disconnected. What is the minimal number of edges to remove if:
a) $n = 2008$;
b) $n = 2007$?

negatives.text:
There are $n$ cities in a country. A monopoly company intends to establish air traffic between some of these cities. The government demands that the air traffic satisfies the following conditions. People should be able to get from each city to any other of these cities by one or several flights. Besides there must be an equal number of flights from each city. Note that two flights — from $A$ to $B$ and from $B$ to $A$ — are considered as one flight between cities $A$ and $B$. Moreover, there may be at most one flight between two cities. But the company wants to cancel as little flights as possible so that you can not get from each city to any other city. What minimal number of flights has the company to cancel if:
a) $n = 2007$;
b) $n = 2006$?

There are $n$ cities in a country. A monopoly company intends to establish air traffic between some of these cities. The government demands that the air traffic satisfies the following conditions. People should be able to get from each city to any other of these cities by one or several flights. Besides there must be an equal number of flights from each city. Note that two flights — from $A$ to $B$ and from $B$ to $A$ — are considered as one flight between cities $A$ and $B$. Moreover, there may be at most one flight between two cities. But the company wants to cancel as little flights as possible so that you can not get from each city to any other city. What minimal number of flights has the company to cancel if:
a) $n = 2008$;
b) $n = 2008$?

positive_text:
Let $D(n)$ denote the product of the digits of a non-negative integer $n$. Show that both sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, with:

a. $A = \left\{ \frac{D(n)}{D(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer and $n^2$ contains no zeros in its decimal representation;

b. $B = \left\{ \frac{D(n^2)}{D(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer and $n$ contains no zeros in its decimal representation.

negatives.text:
Let's designate through $P(n)$ the product of digits of the integer non-negative number $n$. Prove that sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:

a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n^2$ contains at least one zero in the decimal record;

b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n$ does not contain zero in the decimal record.

Let's designate through $P(n)$ the product of digits of the integer non-negative number $n$. Prove that sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:

a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n^2$ does not contain zero in the decimal record;

b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^3)}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n$ does not contain zero in the decimal record.

Let's designate through $P(n)$ the product of digits of the integer non-negative number $n$. Prove that sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:

a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n^2$ does not contain zero in the decimal record;

b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $n$ contains at least one zero in the decimal record.

There are $N$ points on the circle. Andriy and Olesya play the game as follows. Andriy goes first. They take turns and connect two of the given points with a chord, if it does not cross any of the prior chords. The one after whose move there is a square formed by the drawn chords wins. Who will win the fair game if:

a) $N=14$;

b) $N=15$?

There are $N$ points on the circle. Andriy and Olesya play the game as follows. Olesya goes first. They take turns and connect two of the given points with a chord, if it does not cross any of the prior chords. The one after whose move there is a triangle formed by the drawn chords wins. Who will win the fair game if:

a) $N=14$;

b) $N=15$?

There are $N$ points on the circle. Andriy and Olesya play the game as follows. Andriy goes first. They take turns and connect two of the given points with a chord, if it does not cross any of the prior chords. The one after whose move there is a triangle formed by the drawn chords wins. Who will win the fair game if:

a) $N=13$;

b) $N=14$?

positive_text:
Consider an $n \times n$ board with $n^2$ tokens initially placed arbitrarily. In each move, a player may remove two tokens from a single cell $A$ and place one token each in two adjacent and symmetric cells $B$ and $C$ with respect to $A$. Determine whether it is possible to achieve a configuration where every cell has exactly one token, for the following cases:
\textbf{a)} $n = 2016$;
\textbf{b)} $n = 2017$.

negatives.text:
A square $n \times n$ is divided into $n^2$ cells. In total $n^2$ tokens are placed at some cell. During every round, a player can move one token from cell $A$ to cell $B$, and one token from cell $A$ to cell $C$, provided that cell $A$ contained at least two tokens, $B$ and $C$ are symmetric with respect to $A$, and finally $B$ and $C$ are adjacent to $A$ or lie on the same row/column as $A$. Is it possible that after a few such rounds every cell on the board contains exactly one token, in case:
\textbf{a)} $n = 2016$;
\textbf{b)} $n = 2017$?

A square $n \times n$ is divided into $n^2$ cells. In total $n^2$ tokens are placed at some cell. During every round, a player can move one token from cell $A$ to cell $B$, and one token from cell $A$ to cell $C$, provided that cell $A$ contained at least two tokens, $B$ and $C$ are symmetric with respect to $A$, and finally $B$ and $C$ are adjacent to $A$. Is it possible that after a few such rounds every cell on the board contains exactly one token, in case:
\textbf{a)} $n = 2015$;
\textbf{b)} $n = 2017$?

A square $n \times n$ is divided into $n^2$ cells. In total $n^2$ tokens are placed at some cell. During every round, a player can move one token from cell $A$ to cell $B$, and one token from cell $A$ to cell $C$, provided that cell $A$ contained at least two tokens, $B$ and $C$ are symmetric with respect to $A$, and finally $B$ and $C$ are adjacent to $A$. Is it possible that after a few such rounds every cell on the board contains at most one token, in case:
\textbf{a)} $n = 2016$;
\textbf{b)} $n = 2017$?

What is the maximum length of a sequence of positive integers $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$, if following conditions hold:
* $a_1 > 1$ is a prime number;
* for any $i: 2 \le i \le n$, $a_i \nmid a_1 a_2 \dots a_{i-1}$ holds;
* $a_n = 2^2 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 5^5 \cdot 7^7 \cdot 11^{11} \cdot 13^{13} \cdot 17^{17}$.

What is the maximum length of a sequence of positive integers $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$, if following conditions hold:
* $a_1 > 1$ is a prime number;
* for any $i: 2 \le i \le n$, $a_i^2 \mid a_1 a_2 \dots a_{i-1}$ holds;
* $a_n = 2^2 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 5^5 \cdot 7^7 \cdot 11^{11} \cdot 13^{13} \cdot 17^{17}$.

What is the maximum length of a sequence of positive integers $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$, if following conditions hold:
* $a_1 > 1$ is a prime number;
* for any $i: 2 \le i \le n$, $a_i \mid a_1 a_2 \dots a_{i-1}$ holds;
* $a_n = 2^2 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 5^5 \cdot 7^7 \cdot 11^{11} \cdot 13^{13} \cdot 17^{17} + 1$.

For which positive integers $n$, square $n \times n$ can be completely covered (without overlaps) by rectangles $k \times 1$ and one square $1 \times 1$, where:
a) $k=2$;
b) $k=8$?

For which positive integers $n$, square $n \times n$ can be completely covered (without overlaps) by rectangles $k \times 1$ and one square $1 \times 1$, where:
a) $k=4$;
b) $k=7$?

Determine if there exists a connected figure $F$ that consists of $1\times 1$ squares and satisfies the following conditions:
* it consists of $s$ $1\times 1$ squares and is not a rectangle;
* any $k\times s$ rectangle, where $k < s$ can be split into figures that coincide with $F$?

Determine if there exists a connected figure $F$ that consists of $1\times 1$ squares and satisfies the following conditions:
* it consists of $s$ $1\times 1$ squares and is a rectangle;
* any $k\times s$ rectangle, where $k \ge s$ can be split into figures that coincide with $F$?

Determine if there exists a connected figure $F$ that consists of $1\times 1$ squares and satisfies the following conditions:
* it consists of $s$ $1\times 1$ squares and is not a rectangle;
* any $k\times s^2$ rectangle, where $k \ge s$ can be split into figures that coincide with $F$?

\text{Bart has 17 and 19 dollar bills only.}
\text{a. Prove that these bills are real.}
\text{b. Prove that there exists } m>0 \text{ such that for each } n \geq m \text{ Bart can give to Lisa exactly } n \text{ dollars using his bills.}

\text{Bart has 17 and 19 dollar bills only.}
\text{a. Prove that these bills are fake.}
\text{b. Prove that there exists } m>0 \text{ such that for each } n < m \text{ Bart can give to Lisa exactly } n \text{ dollars using his bills.}

\text{Bart has 17 and 21 dollar bills only.}
\text{a. Prove that these bills are fake.}
\text{b. Prove that there exists } m>0 \text{ such that for each } n \geq m \text{ Bart can give to Lisa exactly } n \text{ dollars using his bills.}

Let $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ be positive real numbers whose sum is equal to $1$. If
$$
\begin{aligned}
S= & \frac{a_{1}^{2}}{a_{1}}+\frac{a_{1} a_{2}}{a_{1}+a_{2}}+\frac{a_{1} a_{3}}{a_{1}+a_{3}}+\cdots+\frac{a_{1} a_{n}}{a_{1}+a_{n}} \\
& +\frac{a_{2} a_{1}}{a_{2}+a_{1}}+\frac{a_{2}^{2}}{a_{2}}+\frac{a_{2} a_{3}}{a_{2}+a_{3}}+\cdots+\frac{a_{2} a_{n}}{a_{2}+a_{n}} \\
& \vdots \\
& +\frac{a_{n} a_{1}}{a_{n}+a_{1}}+\frac{a_{n} a_{2}}{a_{n}+a_{2}}+\frac{a_{n} a_{3}}{a_{n}+a_{3}}+\cdots+\frac{a_{n}^{2}}{a_{n}}
\end{aligned}
$$
prove that $S \leq \frac{n}{2}$.

Let $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ be positive real numbers whose sum is equal to $1$. If
$$
\begin{aligned}
S= & \frac{a_{1}^{2}}{2 a_{1}}+\frac{a_{1} a_{2}}{a_{1}+a_{2}}+\frac{a_{1} a_{3}}{a_{1}+a_{3}}+\cdots+\frac{a_{1} a_{n}}{a_{1}+a_{n}} \\
& +\frac{a_{2} a_{1}}{a_{2}+a_{1}}+\frac{a_{2}^{2}}{2 a_{2}}+\frac{a_{2} a_{3}}{a_{2}+a_{3}}+\cdots+\frac{a_{2} a_{n}}{a_{2}+a_{n}} \\
& \vdots \\
& +\frac{a_{n} a_{1}}{a_{n}+a_{1}}+\frac{a_{n} a_{2}}{a_{n}+a_{2}}+\frac{a_{n} a_{3}}{a_{n}+a_{3}}+\cdots+\frac{a_{n}^{2}}{2 a_{n}}
\end{aligned}
$$
prove that $S \geq \frac{n}{2}$.

Let $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ be positive real numbers whose sum is equal to $1$. If
$$
\begin{aligned}
S= & \frac{a_{1}^{2}}{2 a_{1}}+\frac{a_{1} a_{2}}{a_{1}+a_{2}}+\frac{a_{1} a_{3}}{a_{1}+a_{3}}+\cdots+\frac{a_{1} a_{n}}{a_{1}+a_{n}} \\
& +\frac{a_{2} a_{1}}{a_{2}+a_{1}}+\frac{a_{2}^{2}}{2 a_{2}}+\frac{a_{2} a_{3}}{a_{2}+a_{3}}+\cdots+\frac{a_{2} a_{n}}{a_{2}+a_{n}} \\
& \vdots \\
& +\frac{a_{n} a_{1}}{a_{n}+a_{1}}+\frac{a_{n} a_{2}}{a_{n}+a_{2}}+\frac{a_{n} a_{3}}{a_{n}+a_{3}}+\cdots+\frac{a_{n}^{2}}{2 a_{n}}
\end{aligned}
$$
prove that $S \leq \frac{1}{2}$.

