- $1 \leq x_{i} \leq 10$ and $1 \leq y_{i} \leq 10$ for each $i$;
- $x_{i} < x_{i+1}$ for $i=1, \ldots, 9$;
- if $x_{i}=x_{i+1}$, then $y_{i} \leq y_{i+1}$.

\textbf{Problem:}
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
\item If a single vertex (and all its incident edges) is removed from a finite graph, show that the graph's chromatic number cannot increase by more than 1.
\item Show that, for any $n>2$, there are infinitely many graphs with chromatic number $n$ such that removing any vertex (and all its incident edges) from the graph increases its chromatic number.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Problem:}
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
\item If a single vertex (and all its incident edges) is removed from a finite graph, show that the graph's chromatic number cannot decrease by more than 2.
\item Show that, for any $n>2$, there are infinitely many graphs with chromatic number $n$ such that removing any vertex (and all its incident edges) from the graph decreases its chromatic number by at least 2.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Problem:}
\begin{enumerate}[a.]
\item If a single vertex (and all its incident edges) is removed from a finite graph, show that the graph's chromatic number cannot decrease by more than 1, assuming the graph is triangle-free.
\item Show that, for any $n>2$, there are infinitely many triangle-free graphs with chromatic number $n$ such that removing any vertex (and all its incident edges) from the graph decreases its chromatic number.
\end{enumerate}

